1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,041
Lesson 6.5.

2
00:00:02,421 --> 00:00:03,600
Timing models.

3
00:00:04,717 --> 00:00:08,901
The complexity in the previous lesson
counted only the analog evolution.

4
00:00:09,281 --> 00:00:13,861
But the matrix lives in a digital computer,
and the solution must return to it.

5
00:00:14,241 --> 00:00:17,686
An honest timing model adds
three parts: loading the

6
00:00:17,686 --> 00:00:21,530
problem, letting the system
evolve and reading the result.

7
00:00:21,910 --> 00:00:26,090
Today we will see that the first part,
compilation, is often the most expensive.

8
00:00:27,217 --> 00:00:32,418
First, compilation: each of the
d² entries of A goes through a

9
00:00:32,418 --> 00:00:36,948
digital-to-analog converter,
a DAC, to set a coupling.

10
00:00:37,328 --> 00:00:41,164
Second, the analog dynamics:
equilibrate and average, a

11
00:00:41,164 --> 00:00:44,583
time proportional to d
times the relaxation time.

12
00:00:44,963 --> 00:00:48,441
Third, readout: the d
values of the solution go

13
00:00:48,441 --> 00:00:51,920
through an analog-to-digital
converter, an ADC.

14
00:00:52,300 --> 00:00:55,689
Compilation grows as d²;
digital methods do not need

15
00:00:55,689 --> 00:00:58,948
it, because the matrix is
already in their memory.

16
00:01:00,050 --> 00:01:02,657
What sets the relaxation
time in a circuit?

17
00:01:03,037 --> 00:01:06,810
The RC constant, resistance
times capacitance.

18
00:01:07,190 --> 00:01:11,301
With one microsecond, the analog
core is slow compared with loading.

19
00:01:11,681 --> 00:01:14,810
With ten nanoseconds,
typical of a small integrated

20
00:01:14,810 --> 00:01:17,754
circuit, the dynamics is
a hundred times faster.

21
00:01:18,134 --> 00:01:23,508
Paradoxically, the faster the analog
core, the more the digital part weighs.

22
00:01:24,617 --> 00:01:28,910
Let's run numbers with explicit
assumptions: a billion values per

23
00:01:28,910 --> 00:01:33,468
second in the converters, and dynamics
of twenty relaxation times per

24
00:01:33,468 --> 00:01:37,365
dimension, a scale taken from
the simulation in lesson 6.1.

25
00:01:37,745 --> 00:01:42,116
With dimension one thousand and an
RC of one microsecond, loading takes

26
00:01:42,116 --> 00:01:46,118
one millisecond and the dynamics
twenty: loading is five percent.

27
00:01:46,497 --> 00:01:50,110
With ten nanoseconds, the
dynamics drops to two tenths of a

28
00:01:50,110 --> 00:01:53,357
millisecond and loading
becomes eighty-three percent.

29
00:01:53,737 --> 00:01:56,666
The crossover occurs at a
dimension of twenty thousand in

30
00:01:56,666 --> 00:01:59,442
the first case, and of only
two hundred in the second.

31
00:02:00,550 --> 00:02:02,708
The classic solution is to amortize.

32
00:02:03,088 --> 00:02:06,519
If the matrix does not change
and many systems with different

33
00:02:06,519 --> 00:02:09,837
right-hand sides must be solved,
it is loaded once and then

34
00:02:09,837 --> 00:02:12,874
each solution costs only the
dynamics and the readout.

35
00:02:13,253 --> 00:02:18,626
With a hundred right-hand sides, in the
fast case, the time per solution drops

36
00:02:18,626 --> 00:02:23,723
from 1.2 milliseconds to 0.21, and
loading becomes less than five percent.

37
00:02:24,103 --> 00:02:27,030
Conversely, in a Newton
method where the matrix

38
00:02:27,030 --> 00:02:30,143
changes at every step,
compilation is always paid.

39
00:02:31,250 --> 00:02:35,279
Aifer and colleagues built a
complete timing model, with

40
00:02:35,279 --> 00:02:39,523
all digital, conversion and
analog operations, and compared

41
00:02:39,523 --> 00:02:43,048
it with conjugate gradient
and Cholesky on a GPU.

42
00:02:43,428 --> 00:02:46,722
In dimension one hundred, the
thermodynamic method loses.

43
00:02:47,102 --> 00:02:50,918
In dimensions of a thousand and five
thousand it becomes competitive,

44
00:02:50,918 --> 00:02:54,237
especially early on, when an
approximate solution is enough.

45
00:02:54,617 --> 00:02:58,398
And they warn that their model does
not yet include analog imprecision.

46
00:02:59,517 --> 00:03:04,422
To design around compilation: reuse
the matrix with many right-hand sides,

47
00:03:04,422 --> 00:03:08,797
update only low-dimensional parts
instead of reloading everything,

48
00:03:09,177 --> 00:03:13,365
use parallel banks of converters
and on-chip coupling memory.

49
00:03:13,745 --> 00:03:18,178
In lab nine you will add a timing
model and find the crossover.

50
00:03:19,283 --> 00:03:22,439
Common mistakes: reporting
only the analog time.

51
00:03:22,818 --> 00:03:26,624
Assuming a faster core
always helps end to end.

52
00:03:27,004 --> 00:03:30,250
Forgetting that the digital
baseline pays no transfer.

53
00:03:30,630 --> 00:03:34,154
And reloading the whole matrix when
only the right-hand side changed.

54
00:03:35,283 --> 00:03:39,373
In summary: total time is loading
plus dynamics plus readout.

55
00:03:39,753 --> 00:03:44,613
Loading grows as d² and
dominates when the analog core is

56
00:03:44,613 --> 00:03:49,216
fast; in our model, the
crossover is at d* = 20 τ_r B.

57
00:03:49,596 --> 00:03:52,427
Amortizing over many
right-hand sides solves it.

58
00:03:53,550 --> 00:03:57,512
To practice: write the total time
as a function of the dimension.

59
00:03:57,892 --> 00:03:59,039
Find the crossover.

60
00:03:59,419 --> 00:04:02,517
Compute the time per solution
with several right-hand sides.

61
00:04:02,897 --> 00:04:06,863
And explain why a faster core
makes compilation dominate.
